<ToolPage
  kind="guide"
  title="Pitman vs DuPont: Fixed Shifts or the Seven-Day Break?"
  description="The two classic four-crew 12-hour rotations compared: circadian stability versus the monthly week off, 72-hour weeks, day/night flips, and which crews should pick which."
  h1="Pitman vs DuPont"
>
  <p>
    Both of these rotations solve the same staffing problem — four crews, 12-hour shifts,
    round-the-clock coverage at a 42-hour weekly average. They just disagree about what a human
    being should get in exchange. The <a href="/pitman-schedule-calculator">Pitman</a> pays you in
    <strong>stability</strong>; the <a href="/dupont-schedule-calculator">DuPont</a> pays you in
    <strong>time</strong> — one glorious seven-day break every 28 days.
  </p>

  <h2>What your body thinks</h2>
  <p>
    The Pitman keeps crews on fixed shifts: day people stay on days, night people stay on nights,
    inside a 14-day 2-2-3 skeleton. Your circadian rhythm gets one job. The DuPont rotates
    everybody through nights <em>and</em> days inside every 28-day cycle — four nights, three off,
    three days, one off, three nights, three off, four days, then the seven-day break. That single
    off-day between a day block and a night block is the most complained-about 24 hours in shift
    work: not long enough to flip your sleep, short enough to ruin it. If you struggle with sleep,
    that one detail should decide this comparison for you.
  </p>

  <h2>What your calendar thinks</h2>
  <p>
    Pitman hands you a full weekend every other week, forever — the every-other-weekend guarantee
    that makes family scheduling almost normal. DuPont's gift is different: a true seven-day break
    every month <em>without touching leave</em>. DuPont veterans plan their year around those
    breaks — trips, hunting seasons, home projects — and treat vacation days as extenders rather
    than the vacation itself. But DuPont also contains a 72-hour week in every cycle, and that week
    is exactly as brutal as it sounds.
  </p>

  <h2>The overtime fine print</h2>
  <p>
    DuPont's lumpy weeks make paychecks lumpy: the 72-hour week generates a large overtime check
    (32 OT hours), the zero-hour week generates none. Pitman's alternating 36/48 weeks produce a
    steadier rhythm of 8-hour overtime weeks. Under the FLSA, all of it must be paid at your
    regular rate including differentials — run your own week through the
    <a href="/shift-differential-calculator">differential and OT calculator</a> to see what each
    rotation actually deposits.
  </p>

  <h2>Who actually picks which</h2>
  <p>
    Policing, dispatch, EMS, and security lean Pitman — fixed shifts suit operations where crews
    are permanently day- or night-oriented, and the term "Pitman" itself comes from that world.
    Chemical plants, refineries, paper mills, and power stations lean DuPont — heavy industry has
    run it since the 1950s, its crews are used to rotation, and the monthly week off is a
    deeply defended tradition. If your workplace is choosing fresh, the practical questions are:
    <em>Can our people flip between days and nights without falling apart? Is a monthly week off
    worth a 72-hour week?</em>
  </p>

  <h2>See a year of each before deciding</h2>
  <p>
    Abstract descriptions hide the reality of specific dates — which rotation works your
    Thanksgiving, whose break lands on the school holidays, where the 72-hour weeks fall. Generate
    both from one known start date (<a href="/pitman-schedule-calculator">Pitman</a> ·
    <a href="/dupont-schedule-calculator">DuPont</a>), check the holiday answers, and export both
    to your phone before the vote. And whichever wins, the
    <a href="/night-shift-sleep-calculator">night shift sleep calculator</a> will matter more on
    DuPont — build the anchor-sleep plan before your first night block, not after.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
